BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:DEMOCRACY IN DOCUMENTARY 🎟️
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261016T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261016T163000
DTSTAMP:20260918T165146Z
UID:6aa1ba453e8fcdbaebbd2a4f@woodstockfilmfestival.org
DESCRIPTION:As corporate news consolidation accelerates and independent jo
 urnalism faces growing challenges\, documentaries are filling a critical g
 ap. Even as shifts within the documentary industry reshape how nonfiction 
 stories are funded\, distributed\, and seen\, the form remains a powerful 
 force for public understanding and civic engagement. With the 2026 midterm
  elections approaching\, what role can documentaries play in strengthening
  an informed public – and what responsibility do filmmakers have in prot
 ecting democracy?\n\nPanelists:Maite Alberdi (A Child Of My Own)\,Raney Ar
 onson-Rath (Frontline)\, Carl Deal (Steal This Story\, Please!)\,StephenMa
 ing (The Great Experiment)\, Pamela Yates (Borderland: The Line Within)\n
 \nCarl Dealis an independent filmmaker. He directed and producedSteal This
  Story\, Please!\,Citizen Koch\, and Oscar®-nominatedTrouble the Waterwit
 h longtime collaborator Tia Lessin. Deal has also produced and distributed
  several Michael Moore films.\n\nRaney Aronson-Rathis an Oscar®-winning p
 roducer and a leading voice in documentary filmmaking and distribution as 
 editor-in-chief and executive producer ofFrontline\, PBS’ flagship inves
 tigative journalism documentary series. She also leads the GBH Documentary
  Unit\, which includesFrontline\,Nova\, andAmerican Experience. Aronson-Ra
 th has spearheadedFrontline’sexpansion into the theatrical documentary s
 pace with global reach. Producing more than 20 films each year on the defi
 ning issues of our time\, select highlights during her tenure include: win
 ning the Academy Award for20 Days in Mariupoland receiving Academy Award n
 ominations forAbacus: Small Enough to JailandFor Sama.Under her leadership
 \, Frontline Features produced and distributedOne in a Million\,which foll
 ows the ten-year journey of a Syrian refugee and won the Sundance Audience
  Award and jury prize for directing. Aronson-Rath is also a producer onOne
  In A Million.\n\nStephen Maingis an Emmy Award-winning filmmaker. His mos
 t recent films\,Union(P.O.V./Criterion Channel) andCrime + Punishment(Hulu
 /IFC)\, won Special Jury Awards at the Sundance Film Festival and were sho
 rtlisted for the Academy Award for Best Documentary.Crime + Punishmentalso
  received an Emmy Award for Outstanding Social Issue Documentary. He was s
 tory producer and an editor on the AcademyAward®-winning documentaryAll T
 he Empty Rooms(Netflix). His previous films\, which he also directed\, fil
 med and edited –High Tech\,Low Life\,The SurrenderandDirty Gold– have 
 screened internationally\, received numerous awards and were released on P
 OV\, Field of Vision and Netflix\, respectively. Maing is the recipient of
  DOC NYC’s Robert and Anne Drew Award for Documentary Excellence\, the M
 aysles Documentary Center's Albie Award\, fellowships from the Guggenheim 
 Foundation\, United States Artists\, Sundance Institute\, and NBC Original
  Voices\, as well as a World Press Photo Award\, Pictures of the Year Inte
 rnational Award\, and IDA's prestigious Courage Under Fire Award. He has w
 orked as an adjunct professor and filmmaking mentor at numerous universiti
 es and organizations.\n\nMaite Alberdiis a director\, screenwriter\, and p
 roducer from Santiago\, Chile\, considered one of the most notable and inf
 luential voices and talents in contemporary Latin American documentary fil
 mmaking. From her earliest work\, she showed a particular interest in expl
 oring everyday life and discovering\, in the simplest gestures\, fascinati
 ng stories that surpass fiction. Short films such asCarrete Down(2004)\,Lo
 s Trapecistas(2005)\, andLas Peluqueras(2007) anticipated a cinematic pers
 pective that would later fully develop in her debut featureThe Lifeguard(2
 011). The film surprised audiences with its sensitivity and keen observati
 on\, marking the beginning of a distinguished international career. Her re
 cognition grew withTea Time(2014)\, a documentary that became the first wo
 rk of its kind to be nominated for a Goya Award in the Best Ibero-American
  Film category.\n\nAlberdi subsequently directedThe Grown-Ups(2016)\, whic
 h portrays the adult lives of a group of people with Down Syndrome. In 202
 0\, she achieved global reach withThe Mole Agent\, a feature documentary t
 hat earned an Academy Award®nomination for Best Documentary Feature. This
  milestone made Maite Alberdi the first Chilean woman nominated for a docu
 mentary film\, and the film the first Chilean production to compete in tha
 t category.\n\nFour years later\, another of Maite Alberdi's works would m
 ake history again.The Eternal Memory(2023)\, which portrays the story of j
 ournalist Augusto Góngora and his partner\, actress Paulina Urrutia\, aft
 er he receives an Alzheimer's diagnosis\, became the first Chilean film to
  win the Goya Award for Best Ibero-American Film\, and also the highest-gr
 ossing documentary in Chilean cinema history. It received the Grand Jury P
 rize for Best International Documentary at Sundance and an Academy Award n
 omination. Most recently\, Maite Alberdi also directedIn Her Place\, her f
 irst foray into fiction\, which received a Goya Award nomination.A Child o
 f My Own(2026)\, which had its world premiere at the Berlin International 
 Film Festival\, is her first documentary filmed entirely in Mexico.\n\nPam
 ela Yatesis the founder ofSkylight\, a non-profit organization that combin
 es cinematic arts with the quest for justice to inspire the defense of hum
 an rights. She directed the Sundance Film Festival award winningWhen the M
 ountains Tremble\; is the Executive Producer of the Academy Award winningW
 itness to War\;and the Director ofState of Fear: The Truth About Terrorism
 \, which was awarded the Overseas Press Club Award and has been translated
  into 47 languages and broadcast in 154 countries. Her filmGranito\, for 
 which she was awarded a Guggenheim Fellowship\, was used as key forensic e
 vidence in the genocide trial against Efraín Ríos Montt in Guatemala. He
 r third film in the Guatemalan trilogy\,500 YEARShad its world premiere at
  the Sundance Film Festival\, was broadcast on the Public Television serie
 s POV\, and was selected for the Amazon Festival Stars program streamed a
 round the world. Her most recent film that investigates the U.S. border-in
 dustrial complex\,BORDERLAND | The Line Within\,opened in 50 cities across
  the U.S. and is in high demand right now from Minneapolis to Los Angeles
 \, Memphis to Chicago\, as part of the movement to inform\, activate\, and
  strengthen communities aimed at stopping immigration abuses. It was awar
 ded the Poynter Fellowship in Journalism at Yale University and the Inaugu
 ral Art of Activism Award from the Woodstock Film Festival. She is current
 ly in production on the sequel:The Truth Tellers.\n\nModerator:\n\nAmy Goo
 dmanhostsDemocracy Now!\, a daily\, independent\, award-winning news progr
 am airing worldwide\, with millions of subscribers across social media pla
 tforms. She is a bestselling author\, columnist\, and the subject of the d
 ocumentarySteal This Story\, Please!\, by directors Tia Lessin and Carl De
 al.\n\n🎟️ Tickets Available\n\n🔗 https://woodstockfilmfestival.org
 /2026-all-events?eventId=6aa1ba453e8fcdbaebbd2a4f
LOCATION:Utopia Studios\, 293 Tinker Street\, Woodstock\, NY 12498
URL:https://woodstockfilmfestival.org/2026-all-events?eventId=6aa1ba453e8f
 cdbaebbd2a4f
END:VEVENT
